# Rank 1133 / CVE-2025-43971: gobgp's BGP OPEN message SoftwareVersion
# capability parser checked the declared length against the buffer's upper
# bound but never rejected a declared length of zero, so
# `data[1:c.SoftwareVersionLen]` became `data[1:0]`, a slice expression whose
# low bound (1) exceeds its high bound (0), which Go rejects at runtime with
# a panic, crashing the BGP speaker on one attacker-crafted OPEN message
# (CWE-193).
#
# CapSoftwareVersion.DecodeFromBytes in pkg/packet/bgp/bgp.go read
# `softwareVersionLen := uint8(data[0])` and guarded only
# `if len(data[1:]) < int(softwareVersionLen) || softwareVersionLen > 64`,
# an upper-bound check against both the remaining buffer length and a
# fixed cap. Nothing excluded zero, and slicing with a low bound of 1 and a
# high bound taken from an unchecked zero value is invalid Go regardless of
# how much buffer remains.
#
# Pool commit 08a001e06d90 is the real fix (COMMIT_OK, its own message states
# the crash line and reason precisely). It adds
# `|| softwareVersionLen == 0` to the same guard, one clause, no restructuring.
# No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, and a distinct one from the C/C++ index-guard family
# already on record at ranks 1026, 1032, 1038, 1096 and 1102: those are all
# raw-pointer index expressions in C or C++, checked by an analysis that
# exists only for those languages and is already known to be upper-bound-only
# in the sense of never catching a negative (underflowed) index. This bug is
# a Go slice expression, `data[1:N]`, where the failure mode is not an index
# going negative but the slice's own low bound (a literal, 1) exceeding its
# high bound (a variable, N, that can be 0) -- a relation between the two
# operands of one slice expression, not a value going out of a single
# variable's valid range. Go has no index or slice-bound analysis at all in
# this engine; the closest existing issue by theme,
# code.PotentialUnboundedCopy (VYQL-MEM-002), states a copy or write that can
# exceed its destination's capacity, the inverse direction from a slice whose
# own bounds are internally inconsistent, and its condition does not reach
# this shape in any language. Scanning either revision of bgp.go reports 0
# findings.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1133: a declared length of zero produces an invalid slice expression"
  lang go
  file bgp.go
  reject VYQL-MEM-002
  code
  ```
  package bgp

  func (c *CapSoftwareVersion) DecodeFromBytes(data []byte) error {
      c.DefaultParameterCapability.DecodeFromBytes(data)
      data = data[2:]
      if len(data) < 2 {
          return NewMessageError(BGP_ERROR_OPEN_MESSAGE_ERROR, BGP_ERROR_SUB_UNSUPPORTED_CAPABILITY, nil, "Not all CapabilitySoftwareVersion bytes allowed")
      }
      softwareVersionLen := uint8(data[0])
      if len(data[1:]) < int(softwareVersionLen) || softwareVersionLen > 64 {
          return NewMessageError(BGP_ERROR_OPEN_MESSAGE_ERROR, BGP_ERROR_SUB_UNSUPPORTED_CAPABILITY, nil, "invalid length of software version capablity")
      }
      c.SoftwareVersionLen = softwareVersionLen
      c.SoftwareVersion = string(data[1:c.SoftwareVersionLen])
      return nil
  }
  ```

test "rank1133: a declared length of zero is rejected before the slice expression runs"
  lang go
  file bgp.go
  reject VYQL-MEM-002
  code
  ```
  package bgp

  func (c *CapSoftwareVersion) DecodeFromBytes(data []byte) error {
      c.DefaultParameterCapability.DecodeFromBytes(data)
      data = data[2:]
      if len(data) < 2 {
          return NewMessageError(BGP_ERROR_OPEN_MESSAGE_ERROR, BGP_ERROR_SUB_UNSUPPORTED_CAPABILITY, nil, "Not all CapabilitySoftwareVersion bytes allowed")
      }
      softwareVersionLen := uint8(data[0])
      if len(data[1:]) < int(softwareVersionLen) || softwareVersionLen > 64 || softwareVersionLen == 0 {
          return NewMessageError(BGP_ERROR_OPEN_MESSAGE_ERROR, BGP_ERROR_SUB_UNSUPPORTED_CAPABILITY, nil, "invalid length of software version capablity")
      }
      c.SoftwareVersionLen = softwareVersionLen
      c.SoftwareVersion = string(data[1:c.SoftwareVersionLen])
      return nil
  }
  ```
